{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "\"\"\"\nWrite a function that takes in positive integers m and n and finds the number of possible sequences of length n, such that each element is a positive integer and is greater than or equal to twice the previous element but less than or equal to m.\nassert get_total_number_of_sequences(10, 4) == 4\n\"\"\"\n```python\ndef get_total_number_of_sequences(m, n):\n    \"\"\"Count sequences where each element >= 2*previous and <= m.\"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n    if n == 1:\n        return m\n    \n    # dp[i][j] = number of sequences of length i ending with value j\n    dp = [[0] * (m + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    \n    # Base case: sequences of length 1\n    for j in range(1, m + 1):\n        dp[1][j] = 1\n    \n    # Fill dp table\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        for j in range(1, m + 1):\n            # Previous element must be <= j//2\n            for prev in range(1, j // 2 + 1):\n                dp[i][j] += dp[i-1][prev]\n    \n    # Sum all sequences of length n\n    return sum(dp[n][j] for j in range(1, m + 1))\n```"}
